The conductivity of a pure substance depends on intrinsic and extrinsic factors. Intrinsic factors are determined by the nature of the substance which ultimately depends on the mobility of ions.  Anything impeding this movement will create resistance to the current flow. Ionic movement is impacted by inter-ionic interaction, solute-solvent interactions, and solvent interactions. Temperature is an extrinsic factor that also plays a key role in ionic conductivity as high temperatures increase the average kinetic energy of ions hence favoring ionic conduction. The effect of concentration is easily appreciated in isomolar solutions, which are solutions with the same molarity. The more ions in the solution, the higher the conductivity will be. For example, at room temperature, the conductivity of a 0.02M \ce{HCl} solutions is 7m$\text{S}\cdot \text{cm}^{-1}$, whereas the conductivity of a 0.1M \ce{HCl} solutions is 44m$\text{S}\cdot \text{cm}^{-1}$.